Oh, no, it is not. It is to deny to a farmer in an area of six sections or more the right to come under the provisions of this act because of the fact that he is not living in an area which is now said
[Mr. Quelch.}
to be in the same position as a full township in relation to all the terms of this legislation. He is living in an area where the average yield is more than eight bushels to the acre; that is why he is denied this assistance. It has nothing to do with his individual position.

I thank the minister for his answer. Is it contemplated that in such agreements the government may be contributing to educational campaigns to be carried on through such associations? The campaigns of course would be subject to the approval of the minister.
45781-159}
Canada Forestry Act

When this bill was in the resolution stage I moved an amendment suggesting that ice cream should be exempt from excise or sales tax. At that time the parliamentary assistant classed ice cream as a dessert and stated that it would be unfair to exempt it from tax when such things as jellies had to pay the excise or sales tax. Upon making inquiry I find that schedule III of the Excise Tax Act exempts the following, among other foodstuffs:
Bakers' cakes and pies including biscuits, cookies or other similar articles;
Flour including pastry, cake, biscuit and similar mixes;
Fruit, fresh, canned, frozen, dried or evaporated;
Jams, jellies, marmalades, and preserves;
Peanut butter.
If those things are not desserts then somebody has been fooling me for a long time. I submit that ice cream should be exempt. We are faced with surpluses of certain agricultural products for which we are anxious to obtain markets and one way in which surplus cream can be disposed of is in the manufacture of ice cream. I have certain figures which indicate the amount of cream going into ice cream production. The production of ice cream during the current year will require the equivalent of 450 million pounds of milk, whereas the annual average utilization of milk for this purpose during the period 1935-39 amounted to only 115 million pounds. To illustrate the importance of this trend, it might be pointed out that, on the basis of the 1935-39 per capita consumption of ice cream, this increase in utilization represents a milk equivalent of approximately 13 million pounds of butter or 26 million pounds of cheese. Under the circumstances it would appear logical to deduce that this development has made a substantial contribution to the diversion of milk away from products for which there is a contracting demand, thereby reducing the quantities of products which would otherwise be subject to the price support policy of the government.
During the past five or ten years there has been a tremendous increase in the amount of milk used for the production of ice cream, but this has reached a peak and levelled off. At the present time there is a slight reduction taking place in the use of milk for ice cream purposes. There is a surplus of cream and milk products in the country today for which the government will have to take some responsibility. We have some 73 million pounds of butter in cold storage, and 48 million to 50 million pounds of it are under the control of the government. We also have large quantities of powdered milk. There is

a market in Canada for these surpluses, and we are discouraging the use of them by continuing to place a sales tax or excise tax on a food product. It is not a luxury; it is something which every kid uses every day.
